# Review the best valid price after login fix

## Final review scope

The fixes now span two isolated worker branches:

- AI Hero: `96cbb425b48dc2d104712d66eb29efdc62db55c2` on `worker/ppp-login-price`
- Course Builder: `960d15e094fc59efc91f21bf185fb969a683ce15` on `worker/checkout-provider-idempotency`

AI Hero carries the exact Course Builder runtime change as a pnpm patch against `@coursebuilder/core@2.0.3`. This keeps the app commit deployable without publishing a package during this security fix. Course Builder also has the source change and a patch changeset for the next Core release.

Do not merge or deploy `3f30ec4`, `6ebf76a`, or `41991b3` without `96cbb42`.

## Review history

1. `3f30ec4` preserved query country. Review blocked caller-controlled country trust.
2. `6ebf76a` signed country and checkout scope. Review blocked replay and transfer.
3. `41991b3` added durable one-time claims and browser/user binding. Review blocked false provider completion, missing Stripe idempotency, and the absent real MySQL race test.
4. `96cbb42` and Course Builder `960d15e` close those provider and database gates.

## Trusted handoff

`/subscribe/verify-login` ignores query country and signs the trusted Vercel country with a 10-minute HMAC handoff. The payload binds:

- version
- country
- PPP intent
- product ID
- quantity
- issue and expiry timestamps
- random nonce

The browser also receives a 256-bit `__Host-aih_checkout_login_session` cookie with `HttpOnly`, `Secure`, `SameSite=Lax`, host-only scope, path `/`, and a 15-minute lifetime. Only its domain-separated hash is stored.

The first authenticated claim binds the verified user ID. Another browser or user cannot claim or replay the handoff.

## Durable lifecycle

The reviewed lifecycle is:

```text
issued ──claim──> consuming ──verified provider success──> completed
                         ├──retryable failure──> failed_retryable ──retry──> consuming
                         └──terminal failure──> failed_terminal
consuming with expired 30-second lease ──same user/browser──> consuming
issued | failed_retryable ──handoff expiry──> expired / deleted
```

Claim remains one atomic conditional MySQL update. The predicate binds nonce hash, browser hash, signed scope, expiry, state, claim lease, and user. Only one concurrent connection can win.

A 30-second claim lease permits process-loss recovery. The recovery uses the same provider idempotency key, so reclaim cannot create a second Stripe Checkout Session.

Completed rows store both:

- verified Stripe Checkout Session ID
- verified `https://checkout.stripe.com` redirect

Sequential replay returns only that exact receipt. `failed_terminal`, `completed`, and `expired` have no outgoing lifecycle transition.

## Typed provider result

Course Builder now has an additive Stripe-specific result method:

```text
success { providerSessionId, redirect }
failure { code, retryable }
```

The legacy redirect method remains for existing consumers. The AI Hero money path uses only the typed method.

Course Builder no longer forces AI Hero to infer success from a redirect. A provider or pricing failure cannot disguise the AI Hero base or error URL as checkout success.

AI Hero also validates a successful receipt before completion:

- session ID starts with `cs_`
- URL uses HTTPS
- URL host is exactly `checkout.stripe.com`

An invalid success receipt becomes terminal failure and is never stored as completed.

## Provider idempotency and recovery

The provider idempotency key is derived from the durable nonce hash:

```text
aih-login-checkout:<64-character nonce hash>
```

It is stable across claim IDs and retries. Course Builder passes it to:

```ts
stripe.checkout.sessions.create(params, { idempotencyKey })
```

Provider customer, coupon, and promotion mutations use stable operation-specific suffixes. Their expiry timestamps derive from the signed handoff issue time, so retries send identical Stripe parameters.

Recovery behavior:

- Ambiguous timeout after Stripe commit retries with the same key and receives the same session.
- Typed retryable failures release the durable claim.
- Typed terminal failures close the lifecycle.
- Completion-write failure releases the claim when possible.
- If the database cannot record the release, lease expiry permits recovery.
- Retry reconciles the same Stripe operation, then stores the existing session ID and redirect.

## Real MySQL and Drizzle proof

`checkout-login-handoff-store.mysql.test.ts` runs the manual migration against disposable `mysql:8.4` and creates separate production-shaped mysql2/Drizzle connections.

It proves:

- the manual DDL applies
- timestamp(3) and boolean values round-trip exactly
- one row issues
- two separate connections race and exactly one acquires
- the loser reports replay without a second claim
- one completion stores session ID plus redirect
- same-user, same-browser sequential reuse returns the exact receipt
- cross-user and cross-browser attempts fail
- retryable failure permits same-user, same-browser reclaim
- stale consuming lease permits process-loss recovery
- expired handoffs cannot claim
- affected-row handling yields one winner and zero losers

The first disposable run exposed a seven-hour local timestamp drift. The production mysql2 pool now sets `timezone: 'Z'`, matching Vercel and PlanetScale UTC behavior. The exact timestamp assertions then passed.

The container and local Colima VM were stopped after the test. No shared or production database was touched.

## Pricing rules preserved

- Turkey PPP remains `$89.70` and beats the current `$99` alumni price.
- Thailand PPP remains `$104.65`; the current `$99` alumni price wins.
- Forged Turkey query country does not alter a US checkout.
- PPP and alumni do not stack.
- When PPP wins, alumni entitlement is not consumed.
- Stripe country, restricted-purchase country, pricing candidate, and provenance use the verified country and selected best valid price.

